{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 3755 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "448a4c9464da1df9e2ba22ded81d74a97a2a7258548e21b78efd19712257cd8e" :edb/db-before-t 3754 :edb/db-after-t 3755 :edb/tx-data [[13194139537067 50 #inst "2026-10-18T03:48:50.904Z" 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186250379 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186250379 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186250379 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{5}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}+(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(-x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x$; ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x$ only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186250379 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{5}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral I is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}+(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(-x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{7}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186250380 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186250380 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186250380 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186250