tion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\sqrt[3]{-x} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{1+2{x}^{4}}$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186250432 1071 "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$P=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 0.5 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$S=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$T=\\begin{bmatrix}1.5 & -3 & 0 & 1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0.2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186250432 1071 "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186250472 1071 "![](images/a0/a0b39135b78cefe8471c7d120850dde9b53786342679b5ed7f25af9110350607.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-x(x-3)$\n- ${y}^{′}=9-{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)(x-3)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186250472 1071 "![](images/a0/a0b39135b78cefe8471c7d120850dde9b53786342679b5ed7f25af9110350607.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186250472 1074 "![](images/a4/a43217f2af81c1bbacbebd8800b7ebcd4076a32d6ff61782e1fdab4cd182d5ee.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-x(x-3)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=9-{x}^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=3,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=2,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186250472 1074 "![](images/a4/a43217f2af81c1bbacbebd8800b7ebcd4076a32d6ff61782e1fdab4cd182d5ee.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-x(x-3)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=9-{x}^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=3,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=2,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-x(x-3)$ only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186250473 1071 "![](images/17/170ddea2148f369d4feedd14540f234d7946bb6670d1ca6eb4c4ebbbb086a760.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+7)$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x+3)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+5)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186250473 1071 "![](images/17/170ddea2148f369d4feedd14540f234d7946bb6670d1ca6eb4c4ebbbb086a760.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186250473 1074 "![](images/cf/cf77e09457cd6b53da801a6eb876a79712935eba623c851a5b6dafa58887a076.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=-7,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+7)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-7$ and $x=-3.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at $x=-7,$ and also evaluates to zero when $x=0,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(x+3)(x+5)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=-5,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186250473 1074 "![](images/cf/cf77e09457cd6b53da801a6eb876a79712935eba623c851a5b6dafa58887a076.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=-7,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equ