vative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′},$ so this is a first-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186253514 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186253514 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}=-9x,\\,y(1)=-4$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y,\\,y(0)=-3$\n- **III.** ${y}^{″}+y=3$" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186253514 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System ${y}^{′}=-9x,\\,y(1)=-4$ is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}=-9x$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=-4.$\n\n- System ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y,\\,y(0)=-3$ is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=-3.$\n\n- System ${y}^{″}+y=3$ is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-9x,\\,y(1)=-4$; ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y,\\,y(0)=-3$ only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186253514 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}=-9x$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=-4.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=-3.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186253519 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186253519 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1=0$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy}=2$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186253519 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation ${e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1=0$ is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-{e}^{-2y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-{e}^{-2y}.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy}=2$ is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2-\\sqrt{xy} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2-\\sqrt{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1=0$; ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186253519 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-{e}^{-2y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-{e}^{-2y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2-\\sqrt{xy} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2-\\sqrt{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186253520 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186253520 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\