rect answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$ only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256380 1074 "![](images/54/54932bcffbbe793d6e1f730e65570b19cfc3603ddb62eaf0a63ef2e5b0705979.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $x=0$ only.\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-x{y}^{2}$ evaluates to zero both on $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{y}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $x=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. For $y\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y}$ evaluates to zero on $x=0$ only, and for $x\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{x}{y}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256382 1071 "![](images/e8/e8b573c6c2c59123de46346ccfc717fa89a98f7838e643a5e82972d753383588.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256382 1071 "![](images/e8/e8b573c6c2c59123de46346ccfc717fa89a98f7838e643a5e82972d753383588.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256382 1074 "![](images/2f/2f654bc96d27ac53dd501b10c5beb0d1c7d04ff4f643c5044692afac26ca2ba8.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0$ only.\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ gives the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$ only, and for $y\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$ does *not* give the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256382 1074 "![](images/2f/2f654bc96d27ac53dd501b10c5beb0d1c7d04ff4f643c5044692afac26ca2ba8.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0$ only.\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$ only, and for $y\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256390 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,1),(3,3),$ and $(5,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of a vertex of ${S}^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256390 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,1),(3,3),$ and $(5,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of a vertex of ${S}^{′}$, the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n- **I.** $(5,-2)$\n- **II.** $(-2,-5)$\n- **III.** $(3,12)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256390 1074 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-2,-5),(3,12),(5,13).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256390 1074 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-2,-5),(3,12),(5,13).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is “The point $(-2,-5)$”; “The point $(3,12)$” only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256410 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256410 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n- **I.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}$\n- **II.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{6}{7}$\n- **III.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256410 1074 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, equations $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}$, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{6}{7}$, and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$ have no solutions." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256410 1074 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, equations I, II, and III have no solutions." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256411 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256411 1071 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n- **I.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2}$\n- **II.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8}$\n- **III.** $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256411 1074 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $