s, only equation I has no solutions." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256520 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N∣-1\\le x+1\\le 3}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣{x}^{2}\\le -2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256520 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N∣-1\\le x+1\\le 3}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣{x}^{2}\\le -2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A⊆B$\n- **II.** $A⊈B$\n- **III.** $B⊆A$\n- **IV.** $A⊇B$" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256520 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $-1\\le x+1\\le 3,$ that is to say, $-2\\le x\\le 2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={1,2}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}\\le -2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB=∅.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $0\\in A$ but $0\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. The empty set is a subset of every set.\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256520 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $-1\\le x+1\\le 3,$ that is to say, $-2\\le x\\le 2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA={1,2}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}\\le -2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB=∅.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $0\\in A$ but $0\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. The empty set is a subset of every set.\n\n- $A⊇B$ is equivalent to $B⊆A,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A⊈B$; $B⊆A$; $A⊇B$ only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256521 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-1),(1,3),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256521 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-1),(1,3),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n- **I.** $(-3,1)$\n- **II.** $(-3,2)$\n- **III.** $(1,-2)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256521 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,-2),(-3,1),(3,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256521 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${90}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(1,-2),(-3,1),(3,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The point $(-3,1)$”; “The point $(1,-2)$” only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256537 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-4),(1,2),(2,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-3\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256537 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-4),(1,2),(2,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-3\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n- **I.** $(2,1)$\n- **II.** $(4,-2)$\n- **III.** $(-1,2)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256537 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-3\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-3\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-3\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(4,-2),(-2,1),(1,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186256537 1074 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-3\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-3\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-3\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(4,-2),(-2,1),(1,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The point $(4,-2)$” only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256566 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-4,-5),(0,2),(1,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under a counterclockwise rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians about the origin?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186256566 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-4,-5),(0,2),(1,-2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under a counterclockwise rotatio