ix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **II.** $det(A)=-4$\n- **III.** The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259668 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2v+1 \\\\ 4u-v-5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2v \\\\ 4u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement $det(A)=-4$ is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=0\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-2)=8.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving” is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving” only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259668 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2v+1 \\\\ 4u-v-5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2v \\\\ 4u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=0\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-2)=8.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259730 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259730 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n- **I.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-x\\ge 0}$\n- **II.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}$\n- **III.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y+x\\ne 0}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259730 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-x\\ge 0}$ is simply connected. Indeed, it is the half-plane $y\\ge x.$ So,   it's a plane region without holes,\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1}$ is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole (containing all points of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1$).\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y+x\\ne 0}$ is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the straight line $y=-x.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint half-planes and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y-x\\ge 0}$ only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259730 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is simply connected. Indeed, it is the half-plane $y\\ge x.$ So,   it's a plane region without holes,\n\n- Region II is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole (containing all points of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1$).\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the straight line $y=-x.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint half-planes and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259731 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259731 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n- **I.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}$\n- **II.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 1}$\n- **III.** ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,xy\\ne 0}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259731 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 4}$ is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 1}$  is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,xy\\ne 0}$ is *not* simply connected.  The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the coordinate axes.  Thus, the region is formed by four disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 1}$ only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259731 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\n- Region II  is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected.  The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the coordinate axes.  Thus, the region is formed by four disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259752 1071 "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259752 1071 "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n- **I.** ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }0.25{(1.1)}^{n}$\n- **II.** ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{n}$\n- **III.** ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n-1}}{{2}^{n}}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259753 1071 "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nI. $162+108+72+48+⋯$\n\nII. $1-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{27}{64}+⋯$\n\nIII. $-10-9-8.1-7.29-⋯$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259753 1071 "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259753 1074 "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $162+108+72+48+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{108}{162} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $1-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{27}{64}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{4})}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $-10-9-8.1-7.29-⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{-9}{-10} \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, series $162+108+72+48+⋯$, $1-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{27}{64}+⋯$, and $-10-9-8.1-7.29-⋯$ converge." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259753 1074 "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $162+108+72+48+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{108}{162} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $1-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{9}{16}-\\frac{27}{64}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{4})}{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $-10-9-8.1-7.29-⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{-9}{-10} \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, series I, II, and III converge." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259776 1071 "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259776 1071 "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n- **I.** The time it takes for a student to complete math homework\n- **II.** The number of hours you spend jogging in a week\n- **III.** The number of candies in a jar\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259776 1074 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259776 1074 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “The time it takes for a student to complete math homework” does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement “The number of hours you spend jogging in a week” describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\n- Statement “The number of candies in a jar” describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of hours you spend jogging in a week”; “The number of candies in a jar” only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259791 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259791 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n- **I.** $f(x)={e}^{x}$\n- **II.** $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$\n- **III.** $h(x)=x|x|$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259791 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{x}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=x|x|$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions $f(x)={e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=x|x|$ satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259791 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{x}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=x|x|$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259803 1071 "![](images/dc/dca985dc352c45477dbb6ada86ad5a9cfa4404969510191a1955e59b082709fe.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186259803 1071 "![](images/dc/dca985dc352c45477dbb6ada86ad5a9cfa4404969510191a1955e59b082709fe.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259803 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is false, while statement ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/eb/eb4da8143700c7d31e0ceacd09ac76a8d1bcb10379a34dce5b59d48f2ae58c13.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/70/70a473397216589350f63482ab3e36cb1247e7825046c70642254c8514564b00.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ only." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186259803 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\