ns of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ but it has a corner in $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=1-x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $h(x)=|x+1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x+1$ over the given interval. Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only functions $g(x)=|x-1|$ and $h(x)=|x+1|$ satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186262685 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ but it has a corner in $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=1-x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $h(x)=|x+1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x+1$ over the given interval. Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186262686 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3]?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186262686 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3]?$\n- **I.** $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- **II.** $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$\n- **III.** $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x-1|)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186262686 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3].$" 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186262686 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function I satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3].$" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186262687 1071 "![](images/95/95e7e2df02e4694d85be868f348bb65fb6a7bf2c646b89000b9af655f937a12f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186262687 1071 "![](images/95/95e7e2df02e4694d85be868f348bb65fb6a7bf2c646b89000b9af655f937a12f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the co