e $det(T)=-30\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$T$ is **not** a singular matrix” and $det(T)=-30$ are true." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186262913 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186262913 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(R)=0$\n- **III.** $det(R)=1$\n\n \n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186262913 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}|-\\sqrt{2}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0+\\sqrt{2})-\\sqrt{2}(2+1)+2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(R)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186262913 1074 "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}|-\\sqrt{2}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0+\\sqrt{2})-\\sqrt{2}(2+1)+2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(R)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$R$ is a singular matrix” and $det(R)=0$ are true." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186262919 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186262919 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\pi ,$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $(-1,0)$\n- **II.** $(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$\n- **III.** $(1,0)$\n- **IV.** $(1,\\frac{\\pi}{2})$" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186262933 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537067 17592186576411 true] [17592186262933 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $c=-1$ gives a line\n\n- **II.** $c=0$ gives a parabola, excluding the origin\n\n- **III.** $c=1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin" 13194139537067 17592186576411 false] [17592186262933 1074 "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{x} 